# .release-removals — files this repository used to publish and deliberately no longer does.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. A comment-only or blank file
# declares nothing — it is not an allow-all.
#
# A file disappearing from a published tree is indistinguishable, from the outside, from a
# mistake. This is the record that it was not one, and where the contents went.

# ── The rolling status log moved up one directory ─────────────────────────────
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` has been published since v0.8.0. It is not withdrawn: it is now
# `docs/STATUS.md`. Same document, same content, shorter path — if you had a link, drop the
# `status/`.
#
# Why it moved. `docs/status/` holds dated, episodic working notes — handoffs, checkpoints,
# point-in-time snapshots — none of which publish. The rolling log is the opposite: one file,
# edited in place, and the place this repository says out loud where it stands. Keeping both
# kinds in one directory meant the difference between them was a filename somebody had to
# remember. After the move the difference is the path, which can be checked instead of
# recalled.
#
# The directory held this one published file and nothing else, so the move empties the
# published side of it rather than splitting it.
#
# Written as the exact path, not as the `docs/status` prefix: a bare directory entry would
# authorise removing anything ever published beneath it — a standing permission granted for a
# one-time move. This authorises the one file that moved. Reversible by moving it back and
# deleting this line.
docs/status/STATUS.md
